{"meta":{"chapter_title":"AI in Education","title":"AI Literacy for Teachers","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting ai literacy for teachers to TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"04","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 531"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_ai_literacy_for_teachers","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/03/03"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_annotation_signal"],"cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_calibration"],"cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-03":["cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_artifact_signal"],"cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-04":["cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# AI Literacy for Teachers\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting AI Literacy For Teachers, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_ai_in_education\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/03-04-ai-literacy-for-teachers.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for AI Literacy for Teachers: AI Literacy For Teachers, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\n\"AI literacy\" is one of the most over-claimed phrases in professional development, because most of what passes for it is tool training: which buttons to press in a particular app. That kind of training expires the moment the app updates and teaches nothing transferable. Real AI literacy for a teacher is not knowing how to operate a chatbot; it is understanding well enough what these systems are, and are not, to make sound judgments about output you did not write and cannot fully trace.\n\nThis section matters because teachers occupy a specific, high-trust position: students and families assume that what a teacher hands out is accurate. When a teacher passes along AI-generated material without the literacy to vet it, that trust is spent on text a machine assembled from statistical patterns, text that can be fluent and false at the same time. The competency this section builds is the disposition and the procedure to verify, to recognize the boundary of what the tool can actually do, and to model that scrutiny for students, who are watching how their teacher treats AI more than they are listening to any lecture about it.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher attends a one-day workshop on an AI lesson-planning platform, earns a certificate, and tells her department she is now \"AI literate.\" The next week she uses it to generate a unit on the American Revolution, complete with a reading list. The lessons are well-structured and the platform formats everything beautifully. She prints it and teaches from it. Two days in, a parent who is a historian emails: one of the \"primary source quotations\" the AI included was never written by the person it is attributed to, and a recommended book does not exist.\n\nShe had the certificate but not the literacy. The workshop taught her to operate the tool; it did not teach her that the tool predicts plausible-sounding text and will fabricate a quotation or a citation with exactly the same confidence it uses for true ones. Literacy would have meant treating every factual claim and every source as unverified until she checked it. The gap between \"trained on the app\" and \"able to vet its output\" is the entire subject of this section.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAI literacy for teachers rests on understanding one technical fact and building three habits on top of it.\n\nThe technical fact: a generative model is a **next-token predictor**, not a knowledge base. It produces the statistically likely continuation of text. It has no internal notion of true versus false; \"the sky is blue\" and a fabricated citation are generated by the same mechanism, and the model is equally fluent and equally confident about both. This is why **hallucination is not a bug to be patched but a property of how the system works**. Fluency is evidence of nothing except fluency.\n\nFrom that fact, three literacy habits follow.\n\n- **Verification by default.** Treat every factual claim, quotation, statistic, and citation in AI output as unconfirmed until you check it against a real source. The model's confidence is not a signal; confident wrongness is its specialty. Citations are the highest-risk item, because fabricated references look exactly like real ones.\n- **Capability boundaries.** Know what the tool can and cannot do. It can rephrase, summarize, brainstorm, and draft structure well. It cannot reliably recall specific facts, do precise arithmetic, judge whether an interpretation is sound, or \"understand\" a diagram. Asking it for what it cannot do, and trusting the answer, is the literacy failure.\n- **Modeling for students.** Your students will calibrate their own AI use to how they see you use it. A teacher who visibly verifies, says \"let me check that,\" and shows the search that caught a fabrication teaches more AI literacy in one moment than a unit of lecture. Hiding your use, or trusting output uncritically in front of students, teaches the opposite.\n\n**Recent evidence.** Verification is not optional. In a controlled study of AI-generated bibliographies, Walters and Wilder (2023) found 55% of GPT-3.5 citations and 18% of GPT-4 citations were entirely fabricated, and among the non-fabricated citations 43% (GPT-3.5) and 24% (GPT-4) still contained substantive errors. A medical systematic-review analysis (Chelli et al., 2024) found reference-hallucination rates of 39.6% for GPT-3.5 and 28.6% for GPT-4, confirming citation fabrication as a measured, model-general property rather than an anecdote.\n\nThe distinction that organizes all three: **operational skill is knowing how to make the tool produce output; AI literacy is knowing whether to trust it.** The first is a button-pressing skill that any app update can obsolete. The second is professional judgment that transfers across tools and persists. These three habits map onto the UNESCO AI Competency Framework for Teachers (2024): verification and accountability fall under its Ethics of AI and human-centred dimensions, capability boundaries under AI foundations and applications, and modeling under AI pedagogy, so the triad is a recognized competency set rather than a private scheme.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An AI tool returns a tidy, confidently formatted citation for a quotation. Which element is the highest-risk item to verify before using it?\" explanation=\"Citations are the highest-risk item because a next-token predictor produces the form of a citation perfectly while inventing the substance. A fabricated reference looks identical to a real one, and the model is equally confident about both, so confidence is no signal at all.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The font and formatting of the citation\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The existence of the cited source itself, since fabricated references look identical to real ones\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Nothing; a confident, well-formatted citation is reliable\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Only the publication year\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA teacher asks an AI tool, \"Give me five quotations from primary sources about the Industrial Revolution, with citations.\" It returns five tidy, formatted quotations, each with an author, a year, and a source. The operationally-skilled teacher copies them into a slide. The AI-literate teacher runs the verification habit.\n\nShe searches the first quotation verbatim. It appears nowhere. She searches the cited source: the journal exists, but no article by that author in that year exists. Of the five, two quotations are real and traceable, one is a real sentiment misattributed to the wrong person, and two are wholesale fabrications with invented sources. Nothing in the output flagged which was which; all five looked identical in confidence and polish. That is the capability boundary made visible: the model is excellent at producing the form of a citation and unreliable at its truth.\n\nShe keeps the two verified quotations, discards the rest, and then makes the literacy move that matters most: in class, she projects the search where she caught the fabricated citation and walks students through how she did it. The lesson the students remember is not the Industrial Revolution content; it is that their teacher does not trust AI output until she checks it, which is the exact habit she wants them to build. The verification took ten minutes and converted a near-miss into the most important thing she taught that week.\n\n## Worked Decision: Verifying an AI-Drafted Lesson\n\nFollow a teacher's annotated before/after judgment while building AI literacy with a lesson-generator. The transcript step is real: she asked the model to draft a photosynthesis quiz with sources.\n\n1. **Before (literacy as button skill):** \"I completed the app training, so I am AI literate. The model gave me five quiz items and three cited studies, all formatted confidently, so I will print them.\" The only evidence is fluent output and a finished training.\n2. **Accuracy check:** Annotate the verification. She searches the three DOIs in the library database. Two are fabricated; the journals exist but the articles do not. The confident tone hid the hallucination.\n3. **Limit named:** Annotate the capability boundary. One quiz item asks students to explain why the model \"understands\" the diagram. She rewrites it, because the model predicts text and does not understand meaning.\n4. **After (literacy made visible):** \"I will use the two verified items, drop the fabricated citations, and replace them with sources I confirmed. In class I will show students the search where I caught the fake DOIs, so they learn to check AI output themselves. I reject the idea that finishing the training made me literate.\"\n5. **Evidence trail:** The revision is justified by **teacher professional judgment** (verify, then decide) and by the database search, not by the model's confidence. The rejected items and citations are recorded with reasons.\n\nStep 1 to step 4 captures the section's lesson: AI literacy is the judgment to verify, name limits, and model critical use, not the ability to operate one tool or to trust fluent output.\n\nNow make the literacy calls yourself. The branching scenario below hands you an AI-drafted lesson with confident output; decide what to trust, what to check, and what to model for students.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Vetting an AI-drafted lesson before teaching it\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"The model returns five quiz items and three cited studies, all confidently formatted. You finished the app training. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Print and teach it; the training made me AI literate\",\"next\":\"print\",\"feedback\":\"A certificate measures attendance, not judgment. Fluent output is evidence of nothing but fluency.\"},{\"label\":\"Treat every factual claim and citation as unverified until checked\",\"next\":\"verify\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Verification by default is the core literacy habit.\"},{\"label\":\"Ask the model to confirm its own accuracy and trust the answer\",\"next\":\"selfcheck\",\"feedback\":\"The tool cannot evaluate its own correctness; its self-assurance is worthless.\"}]},\"print\":{\"prompt\":\"A parent emails that one cited source does not exist. Reconsider your approach?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Search every citation in the library database before reuse\",\"next\":\"verify\",\"feedback\":\"You moved from operating the tool to vetting its output.\"}]},\"selfcheck\":{\"prompt\":\"The model cheerfully affirms it is accurate. Is that enough?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No; check the citations against a real source yourself\",\"next\":\"verify\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Self-affirmation is a capability-boundary failure in miniature.\"}]},\"verify\":{\"prompt\":\"You search the three DOIs. Two journals exist but the articles do not. One quiz item asks students why the model understands the diagram. What is your decision?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep the verified item, drop the two fabricated citations, and rewrite the understands item\",\"next\":\"literate\",\"feedback\":\"You verified, named the capability boundary, and kept only what survives a check.\"},{\"label\":\"Keep all items; they are formatted consistently\",\"next\":\"unsafe\",\"feedback\":\"Consistency of format is not accuracy; you would hand students fabrications.\"}]},\"literate\":{\"prompt\":\"Defensible outcome: you verified each claim, named what the tool cannot do, and you will show students the search that caught the fake DOIs so they learn the habit.\",\"choices\":[]},\"unsafe\":{\"prompt\":\"Non-defensible outcome: you distributed fabricated citations and an item premised on the tool understanding meaning, which it cannot.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore any AI-generated material reaches students, run the literacy check:\n\n1. **Identify the factual claims, quotations, and citations.** These are the high-risk elements; mark every one.\n2. **Verify each against a real source.** Do not let the model's confidence substitute for a check; citations especially must be confirmed to exist.\n3. **Test against the capability boundary.** Did you ask the tool for something it cannot reliably do (recall a specific fact, judge an interpretation, compute precisely)? If so, distrust that part by default.\n4. **Decide what to model.** When students will use the same tool, plan how you will show your verification, not just hand them vetted output.\n\nThe standard is simple: you are accountable for everything you distribute, so unverified AI output is not yet ready to teach from.\n\nOrder the literacy check. Arrange these moves into the sequence a teacher runs before any AI-generated material reaches students.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Identify and mark every factual claim, quotation, and citation\",\"Verify each marked element against a real source\",\"Test the output against the capability boundary (recall, judgment, arithmetic)\",\"Distrust by default anything the tool cannot reliably do\",\"Plan how to model the verification visibly for students\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common misreading equates AI literacy with knowing a tool's interface, which is operational skill, not literacy. A second is trusting fluency: because the output reads smoothly and confidently, it must be accurate, when fluency and accuracy are produced by the same indifferent mechanism. A third is assuming citations are safe because they look formatted and specific; fabricated references are the model's most convincing forgery. A fourth is believing literacy is private, a way to use AI quietly behind the scenes, when modeling visible verification for students is one of its highest-value uses. Finally, \"I completed the training\" is not literacy; a certificate measures attendance, not judgment.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the literacy check to a realistic task: a science teacher uses an AI tool to draft a differentiated reading on cell respiration at three levels.\n\nIdentify the high-risk elements first. The structure and the simplified phrasings are low-risk; the model is good at rephrasing. The factual claims (the steps of respiration, the ATP yield numbers, any \"fun fact\") are high-risk. She checks the ATP count, a place where models notoriously produce plausible but wrong numbers, and finds the draft says 38 where the modern accepted figure is around 30 to 32 (the older 36 to 38 was a theoretical maximum that assumed 3 ATP per NADH and 2 per FADH2; revised P/O ratios of about 2.5 and 1.5 give the lower net count, and direct flux measurements put the cap near 30 to 33; Mookerjee et al., 2017). The model generated a number that \"sounds textbook\" rather than one that is current, which is exactly why a next-token model trained on legacy text reproduces it.\n\nTest the capability boundary. She had also asked it to \"make sure this is accurate,\" and it cheerfully affirmed accuracy, which is the boundary failure in miniature: the tool cannot evaluate its own correctness, so its self-assurance is worthless. She corrects the ATP figure, verifies the respiration steps against her actual textbook, and keeps the differentiated phrasing, which was the tool's genuine strength. Then she plans the modeling move: she will show students both the AI's \"38\" and the verified figure and ask them why a confident source can still be wrong. The tool saved her the tedious differentiation work and, once she vetted it, became a teaching example about verification itself.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of this topic never confuses smooth output with trustworthy output, and never confuses tool fluency with literacy. As you read, keep a two-column note with the **AI claim or capability** on the left and the **verification or boundary** on the right. If you cannot fill the right column for a factual claim, you cannot yet hand that claim to students.\n\nA strong analysis can answer four questions about any AI-generated teaching material:\n\n1. Which elements are factual claims, quotations, or citations, and have I verified each?\n2. Did I ask the tool to do something outside its reliable capability?\n3. How would I demonstrate this verification to students rather than hide it?\n4. What about my judgment here would still hold if the tool or its interface changed?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour prompt transcript is strong when it shows verification and capability reasoning, not just clever prompting. Record which outputs you accepted, which you rejected, and the specific check (the search that found the fake citation, the source that corrected the number) behind each call. The weakest transcripts show a teacher getting the tool to produce polished material; the strongest show a teacher catching what the tool got wrong and deciding what to trust.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact would read: \"I accepted the tool's lesson structure and differentiated phrasing because rephrasing is within its reliable capability; I rejected two of its quotations after a database search showed the cited sources do not exist, and I will show students that search so they learn the same habit.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove from a content task to an interpretive one: a teacher asks an AI tool to evaluate a student's essay and suggest a grade. The verification habit does not transfer cleanly, because now the problem is not a checkable fact but a capability boundary. Judging whether an argument is sound is exactly what a next-token predictor cannot reliably do, so the literate response is not \"verify the grade\" but \"recognize that this is outside the tool's competence and refuse to delegate it.\" Knowing when the right move is to verify and when the right move is to not use the tool at all, because the task lies past its boundary, is the deeper form of AI literacy that this case tests.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_in_education\",\"cat531_supplement_ai_literacy_for_teachers\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/03-04-ai-literacy-for-teachers-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"03.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions for CAT 531 03.04. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_ai_literacy_for_teachers\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace records evidence source, risk, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates professional judgment from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). Technological pedagogical content knowledge: A framework for teacher knowledge. Teachers College Record, 108(6), 1017-1054. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- UNESCO. (2024). AI competency framework for teachers. Paris: UNESCO. https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/ai-competency-framework-teachers\n- Chan, C. K. Y. (2023). A comprehensive AI policy education framework for university teaching and learning. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education, 20, 38. https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-023-00408-3\n- Walters, W. H., & Wilder, E. I. (2023). Fabrication and errors in the bibliographic citations generated by ChatGPT. Scientific Reports, 13, 14045. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-41032-5\n- Chelli, M., et al. (2024). Hallucination rates and reference accuracy of ChatGPT and Bard for systematic reviews: Comparative analysis. Journal of Medical Internet Research, 26, e53164. https://doi.org/10.2196/53164\n- Mookerjee, S. A., et al. (2017). Quantifying intracellular rates of glycolytic and oxidative ATP production and consumption using extracellular flux measurements. Journal of Biological Chemistry, 292(17), 7189-7207. https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M116.774471\n- Selwyn, N. (2014). Distrusting Educational Technology: Critical Questions for Changing Times. Routledge. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315886350\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2019). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. Learning, Media and Technology, 45(3), 251-269. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_literacy_for_teachers","stem":"You ask a chatbot for three peer-reviewed studies supporting a teaching strategy. It returns three citations with authors, journals, and DOIs, written confidently. What does AI literacy require next?","options":["Use them, because the confident, complete format shows they are real.","Verify each citation in a library database, because models routinely fabricate plausible-looking references.","Use them if the journal names sound familiar.","Trust them because the model had access to a lot of data."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Fluent, confident formatting is not evidence of accuracy. Models hallucinate citations, so verification against an authoritative source is the literate move.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_4_confident_tone_equals_correct","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_ai_literacy_for_teachers","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_literacy_for_teachers","stem":"A teacher finishes a one-hour training on a lesson-generator app and writes, \"I am now AI literate.\" Which statement best describes the gap?","options":["There is no gap; knowing the app's buttons is AI literacy.","Operating one app is tool skill; AI literacy also includes judging when the tool fits, what it cannot do, and how it affects students.","AI literacy only means typing faster prompts.","The training made her literate in all AI tools."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI literacy is judgment about fit, limits, and impact, not operation of a single product. Tool skill is necessary but not sufficient.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_4_literacy_is_button_skill","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_ai_literacy_for_teachers","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-01","cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-02","cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_literacy_for_teachers","stem":"A student asks, \"Does the AI actually understand my essay when it gives feedback?\" Which response reflects accurate AI literacy?","options":["Yes, it reads and understands meaning like a human reader.","No, it predicts likely feedback from text patterns; it does not understand your argument, so weigh its comments against your own judgment.","Yes, because it was trained on millions of essays.","It understands grammar but feels emotion about your topic."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Generative models pattern-match plausible text; they do not comprehend or verify meaning. Naming this limit is core AI literacy and shapes how output is used.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_4_more_data_means_understanding","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_ai_literacy_for_teachers","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_4_ai_literacy_for_teachers_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_literacy_for_teachers","stem":"A teacher uses AI to draft a quiz, silently fixes two wrong answer keys, and hands students the corrected quiz without comment. From an AI-literacy-instruction standpoint, what was the missed opportunity?","options":["Nothing; students should never see AI involved.","By hiding the verification, she missed the chance to model the critical checking that teaches students to evaluate AI output themselves.","She should have used the AI answers as-is to save time.","She should have told students the AI is always right."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Teacher AI literacy is most powerful when made visible. Modeling how she caught and corrected the errors teaches the critical-use habits that are the goal.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_4_literacy_not_modeled","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_ai_literacy_for_teachers","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-03-04-lo-02"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_4_confident_tone_equals_correct","pattern":"model_confidence_mistaken_for_accuracy","description":"Assuming an AI answer is correct because it is stated fluently and confidently","trigger":"teacher accepts an AI-generated fact, citation, or standard alignment without verifying it","feedback":"Generative models state wrong answers with the same confidence as right ones and can fabricate citations. Verify any factual or standards claim against an authoritative source before using it with students.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_4_literacy_is_button_skill","pattern":"ai_literacy_reduced_to_tool_operation","description":"Equating AI literacy with knowing how to operate a specific app or write prompts","trigger":"teacher claims AI literacy after learning the buttons of one product","feedback":"AI literacy is the judgment to decide when a tool fits, what it cannot do, and how it affects students. Operating an app is necessary but not sufficient.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_4_more_data_means_understanding","pattern":"pattern_matching_mistaken_for_understanding","description":"Believing a model understands meaning rather than predicting likely text from patterns","trigger":"teacher attributes reasoning or intent to a model's output","feedback":"Generative AI predicts plausible continuations; it does not understand content or check truth. Frame its output as a draft to evaluate, not knowledge to trust.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_4_literacy_not_modeled","pattern":"teacher_skips_modeling_critical_use","description":"Treating teacher AI literacy as private rather than something to model openly for students","trigger":"teacher uses AI but hides the verification and judgment steps from students","feedback":"Make your verification visible. Modeling how you check, accept, and reject AI output teaches students the critical-use habits that are the point of AI literacy.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"aaad247a94bc76bdb4724c040d3b81cf129069d8ccc4dc5144162d502bab0c70","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}